MENTOR: Here's **Title** at 1 s, halfway through its first move. "An easing lesson" is still faint because Opacity is only part of the way up, and Position and Scale are mid-move as well.

At the moment all four pairs are **linear**. From 0.5 s to 1.5 s, Title's Position, Scale and Opacity all change at one constant speed, then stop dead at 1.5 s. Position then moves on to 3 s at a new constant speed. Constant speed with sudden starts and stops is what makes motion look mechanical.

**Easing** makes the layer speed up gently out of a key and slow down into the next one, the way real objects move.

How to do it:
1. Select **Title** and press **U** to show its keyframes.
2. Select the keys you want to ease. You can drag a box around several at once.
3. Choose **Animation > Keyframe Assistant > Easy Ease**, or press **F9**.
4. The diamond keys change to an hourglass shape. To check the result, open the **Graph Editor** (the little graph button above the timeline). Linear motion shows up as flat lines with sharp corners in the speed graph, and eased motion as smooth hills.

**Your turn:** ease all four Title pairs:
- Position, 0.5 s → 1.5 s
